e of  syrian refugees it had a similar effect  in europe where more than a million  syrians sought safety in 2015 but as  redmond shannon explains the potential  for another refugee crisis from  afghanistan is being met differently  from the eastern border of poland to the  greek frontier with turkey parts of the  european union are putting up walls both  physically and politically if we invite  others from outside europe that will  change the cultural identity of europe  there are some countries who accept it  hungary is not among the countries most  of the 27 nations in the eu were  involved in the afghan war and were part  of the scrambled evacuation effort like  canada they still hope to help those  left behind but eu governments failed to  agree in a plan for all countries to  take in vulnerable afghans under their  so-called burden-sharing migration pact  the absolute red lines for the migration  skeptics in these conclusions were  putting anything in there about an  expectation on  numbers  in terms of how many  new people europe would resettle instead  governments could only agree to talk of  bolstering borders and providing aid for  refugees in countries bordering  afghanistan we need to avoid a  humanitarian crisis  we need to avoid a migratory crisis the  main reason for this is the memory of  the chaos of 2015 when more than one  million syrians fled a civil war and  headed for europe greece on europe's  eastern edge is still dealing with the  consequences and i think what happened  in 2015 was a mistake we acknowledge it  openly  we address the need to support refugees  closer  to the source of the problem which is  afghanistan since 2016 the eu has  effectively paid turkey to keep syrians  from getting to greece it has also lost  the uk as a member following a  referendum campaign dominated by  immigration germany took in hundreds of  thousands of syrians but most candidates  to replace angela merkel as chancellor  in this month's election insist there